 - **War target selection — distance guard**: added a **distance-based pre-filter** in `Risk::preEvaluate` (WarUtilityAspect.cpp) so the AI **rejects faraway rivals** during war-risk evaluation. This curbs "bait" wars that leave cores under-defended and keeps armies concentrated near relevant fronts. See [KI#61](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#61---seemingly-tremendously-improved-ais-getting-baited-to-attack-faraway-rivals-leaving-their-core-cities-mostly-defenseless-but-and-oftentimes-dying-shortly-after-to-an-opportunistic-rivals-changed-by-adding-a-pre-filter-reject-in-war-risk-assessment-based-on-distance-to-us-in-riskpreevaluate-in-warutilityaspectcpp) and [KI#183](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#183---fixed-advciv-sas-faraway-war-risk-hard-reject-could-make-mediocre-wars-look-extremely-good-by-penalizing-the-peace-scenario).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6036+) **War target selection — distance guard** - Update: **Faraway-war Risk reject no longer inflates UWAI war utility: a follow-up BBAI test in save file 450 showed the older AdvCiv-SAS KI#61 hard reject could also fire in the recursive peace scenario. Since UWAI scores war as `war scenario - peace scenario`, this could turn a mediocre war into an absurdly attractive one, e.g. `warScenarioUtility=-220`, `peaceScenarioUtility=-105386`, `finalUtility=105166`. The hard reject no longer applies to the recursive peace scenario; retesting removed all huge positive final-utility artifacts (`positive final: 0`) while preserving the intended negative reject for bad faraway/unreachable war plans. See [KI#183](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#183---fixed-advciv-sas-faraway-war-risk-hard-reject-could-make-mediocre-wars-look-extremely-good-by-penalizing-the-peace-scenario).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6052+) Update 2 : later BBAI logging showed that the guard was too invasive after war had already begun. In save file 450, it forced Sumer to make peace after only 2 turns despite capturing New York with twice Lincoln's power, merely because the next enemy city was beyond the distance limit. It is now restricted to pre-war evaluations, leaving normal UWAI logic to decide when an ongoing war should end.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6035+) **UWAI target choice avoids faraway wars:** Following [KI#61](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#61---seemingly-tremendously-improved-ais-getting-baited-to-attack-faraway-rivals-leaving-their-core-cities-mostly-defenseless-but-and-oftentimes-dying-shortly-after-to-an-opportunistic-rivals-changed-by-adding-a-pre-filter-reject-in-war-risk-assessment-based-on-distance-to-us-in-riskpreevaluate-in-warutilityaspectcpp)'s related fix, BBAI war-target logging in save file 450 showed the AI could still prepare some avoidable faraway wars: for example, on turn 182, Arabia (Team 0) could ignore closer Holy Rome (Team 6) at distance 5, attitudeValue -2, and targetPowerPercent 64, then prepare against the farther Netherlands (Team 5) at distance 10, attitudeValue +1, and targetPowerPercent 92. That is dangerous because a faraway war can split armies from the core, slow reinforcements, expose cities to opportunistic invasions, or make captures harder to hold. The new guard only optionally promotes a nearby land-war target, not an island/naval target, when it is close enough, weak enough, disliked/neutral enough, and has minimal UWAI drive; With the current distance threshold, it only steps in when the alternative target is at least 2 tiles farther away; targets at the same distance or closer are left to normal UWAI. This is intentionally a narrow blunder-fix rather than a war-logic overhaul, so UWAI choices do not become too bland or broadly overridden. In the confirming BBAI run, the guard found 32 valid closer targets and blocked 17 farther alternatives, leaving usual UWAI logic to either pick these closer targets or continue normally if no suitable target remained. See [KI#182](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#182---fixedimproved-uwai-war-target-selection-could-fall-through-to-farther-targets-even-when-a-closer-weakdisliked-land-target-was-available).
-- **City evacuation — doomed city auto-retreat (land units only)**: in `CvUnitAI::AI_evacuateCity`, the AI now optionally (default ON, recommended) **evacuates 100% of its land units** from a **doomed city** (i.e. a city that exceeds the existing `AI_EVACUATION_THRESH`-based (default as of now: 138%) attacker/defender strength threshold (note: the threshold is raised for important cities, as it is worth defending them and generally bad to evacuate them)). Fixed an inherited Base AdvCiv percentile inversion that instead gave this important-city protection to low-value cities; see [KI#172](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#172---fixed-base-advciv-bug-inverted-city-value-percentile-gave-intended-important-city-evacuation-protection-to-low-value-cities). Previously it often retreated only a tiny fraction (e.g., 2 of 11 defenders vs a 22+ stack, whereas now all 11 defenders would evacuate if the city is deemed doomed). This change might prolong wars/game length, but the intent is a **more competitive, less self-sabotaging** AI. See [KI#62](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#62---extremely-betterstronger-ai-almost-not-evacuating-at-all-doomed-cities-2-swordsmen-in-an-11-unit-defending-stack-vs-a-22-attacking-unit-stack-fixed-by-always-and-100-evacuating-city-doomed-city-regardless-of-land-unit-type-in-cvunitaiai_evacuatecity).
-- **Minimum Stack size before war**: Added a minimum stack unit count requirement to prevent the stack from being marked as "ready" until it has enough units: no `MOVE_DECLARE_WAR` flag, no premature advance toward the city, and AI keeps building units until the stack is big enough. Helps fix the **issue of "back and forth" war declarations with immediate retreat without city attack**: low-numbered unit stacks were especially sensible to power swings (city defenses, one more enemy units skews win chance, etc.). Change in `CvUnitAI::AI_attackCityMove`. See [KI#104](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#104---tremendously-improved-base-advciv-issue-of-the-weird-back-and-forth-of-declaring-war-moving-a-stack-then-withdrawing-without-attacking-which-is-extremely-inefficient).
-- **City attack oscillation — pre-check vs strong targets (possible mitigation)**: In `CvUnitAI::AI_attackCityMove`, add a sanity check to **skip marching to/attacking a city** when the **defending stack is too strong**. Goal: prevent the inefficient **advance → abort → backtrack** loop and reduce the risk of being caught without time to retreat. **Note:** in the test sample, this behavior also resolved without the change by running ~2+ autoplay turns in a row instead of 1 turn only from a slightly older save file rather; treat this as a **possible fix/sanity check** (not (at least not yet) broadly tested). See [KI#63](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#63---possibly-prevented-and-possibly-fixed-weird--very-inefficient-back-and-forth-of-going-to-attack-a-city-stack-and-then-going-back-after-seeing-enemy-stack-is-too-strong-i-dont-know-if-this-change-fixes-it-since-the-issue-was-solved-without-it-2-autoplay-turns-were-needed-at-least-it-seems-not-1-but-maybe-this-change-in-cvunitaiai_attackcitymove-helps-a-lot-but).
 - **Early-game razing — distance-aware captures**: Updated conquer/raze logic so that, for roughly the first **~120 turns** (scaled by game speed), the AI **razes captured cities** that aren’t **close enough** to justify early investment, in `CvPlayerAI::AI_conquerCity`. This addresses the base AdvCiv pattern where forces overextend (most troops sit in a faraway capture while **core cities** thin out) and also preserves scarce early economy (city maintenance, low pop, time to build improvements/culture). Indirect effect: less fog-busting far from us → **more barbarians** there, which tends to pressure rivals. Net: a **stronger, more focused** early AI (fewer "split in two" empires). See [KI#64](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#64---greatly-enhanced-ai-not-razing-faraway-cities-at-captures---now-we-raze-them-if-we-are-in-the-early-game-and-they-are-not-close-enough-thanks-to-changes-and-tweaks-in-cvplayeraiai_conquercity).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6042+) **Captured-city razing avoids destroy-then-resettle waste:** BBAI testing in save file 450 showed AIs repeatedly destroy a city and then soon spend a Settler to rebuild on the exact same `(38,50)` plot: Ur was razed, Tours was founded there and razed, then HRE founded Augsburg there only four turns later and eventually razed it too. This wasted population, buildings, Settlers, regrowth, and military effort. The cause was Base AdvCiv's `AI_isAwfulSite`, which misevaluated and judged existing cities through a separate crude terrain veto that could conflict with how Settlers valued the same site. AdvCiv-SAS replaces it with `AI_isSASCityLikelyToBenefitUsLongTerm`, whose intrinsic-quality component reuses the shared SAS low-food and potential-yield helpers: close cities with enough long-term food or workable potential can be kept even without standout bonuses. In the confirming run, HRE kept Ur when it captured the city at population 3; Ur later reached population 13, built a corporation building, and generated a Great Engineer, supporting a stronger AI outcome instead of another wasteful raze/resettle cycle. See [KI#186](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#186---fixedimproved-base-advciv-ai_isawfulsite-could-cause-economically-wasteful-captured-city-razeresettle-cycles-and-destroy-valuable-population-and-buildings).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6042+) **Excessively faraway captured cities are rejected regardless of intrinsic site quality:** the same BBAI investigation exposed a separate strategic problem: a city can have excellent food, bonuses, population, and developed plots yet still be a poor long-term acquisition when it is too far from our nearest city on the same land area. Defending it can exhaust units and split the army from the core; if a nearer rival then captures it, that rival receives an already-developed city without paying for its Settler or initial growth. As for us, we lose diverted investments and end up thinner and weaker. `AI_isSASCityLikelyToBenefitUsLongTerm` therefore applies a tunable same-area distance limit independently of intrinsic quality. Razing such an outpost lets the AI take the conquest gold and move on, avoiding its ongoing maintenance and defensive commitment, preserving more military flexibility, and forcing rivals to spend their own Settler and development time. In the confirming run, Yue-Chi at same-area distance 17 and repeatedly conquered Orleans at distance 10 were razed, and neither location was resettled nearby through turn 300. Save-file 453 also confirmed that even an attractive, seemingly moderate-distance capture can be a trap: temporarily raising the tunable limit from 8 to 9 made Inca keep population-17 Cordoba as a possible bridgehead, but two immediate Ottoman captures killed 10 advanced Incan units and left the developed city transferred back to Spain, so the tested default as of now remains 8. See [KI#186](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#186---fixedimproved-base-advciv-ai_isawfulsite-could-cause-economically-wasteful-captured-city-razeresettle-cycles-and-destroy-valuable-population-and-buildings) and [KI#186.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1862---fixedimproved-base-advciv-active-world-wonder-exemption-could-make-the-ai-retain-remote-captured-cities-exhaust-defenders-and-return-the-preserved-wonder-to-a-nearer-rival).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6043+) **Active-World-Wonder exemption no longer protects unsustainable faraway captured cities:** BBAI logging in a follow-up save-file 450 run showed the concrete failure. England captured the faraway, population-18 city of Elephantine, but an inherited Base AdvCiv rule automatically protected captured cities containing active World Wonders from being razed, even though the city was too far away to benefit its new owner long-term: England committed 15 defenders, but eventually lost at least 7 of them there plus lost Elephantine back to nearby Egypt after only 6 turns anyway. So the AI now optionally disables this Wonder-based protection for faraway cities while preserving it for captures close enough to be sustainable (default because likely stronger and as testing showed too). Razing lets the AI take the conquest gold, permanently deny the Wonder to rivals, and avoid the city's maintenance and defensive commitments and potential future losses and risks, instead of exhausting its army to preserve the Wonder briefly for the nearer rival likely to retake it. Save file 450's run diverged so it could not be reproduced after fix, but Save-file 453 directly confirmed the new path when Inca razed population-17 Madrid at distance 13 despite its active Statue of Zeus. See [KI#186.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1862---fixedimproved-base-advciv-active-world-wonder-exemption-could-make-the-ai-retain-remote-captured-cities-exhaust-defenders-and-return-the-preserved-wonder-to-a-nearer-rival).
-- **Early defender overbuild → switch to versatile offense (AI)**: Heuristic in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit` reduces **early-game overproduction of pure defenders** (especially Longbowmen) by swapping the build to a more **offensive/versatile** unit when defense is already sufficient — e.g., Spearmen, Axemen, Camel Archers, with a preference for civ-specific units like War Chariots when available. Also caps/avoids siege (esp. Trebuchets) when they’re inefficient defensively; production can drop **down to zero** if conditions warrant. If no good replacement exists, the AI can **reflag** the chosen defender to an **attack/attack_city/counter** `UnitAI` instead. In testing, this produced **fewer pure defenders** and **more varied stacks** (e.g., a run where Hatshepsut shifted from ~30 Longbowmen at T130 to ~32 War Chariots, plus Camel Archers). See [KI#53.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#532---fixed-and-enhanced-joao-ai-building-way-too-many-defenders-36-longbowmen-at-turn-130-almost-all-with-unitai_city_defense-or-similar-while-being-boxed-in-with-only-3-cities-instead-of-making-about-half-of-these-offense-units-or-versatile-ones-to-punish-overextending-thin-neighbour-fixed-by-having-an-early-phase-we-have-expectedly-too-few-cities-at-this-stage-of-the-game-no-need-to-overbuild-anymore-denders-at-this-stage-of-the-game-switch-to-offense-or-versatile-unitai-rather-and-our-military-composition-has-way-too-many-defender-unitais-switch-to-offense-or-versatile-unitais-which-seemingly-fix-the-issue) and [KI#53.2.2](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#5322-seemingly-greatly-enhanced-ai-overproducing-defenders-early-on-especially-longbowmen-but-not-only-replaced-with-an-early-produce-more-versatile-units-especially-civ-specific-ones-when-defended-enough-at-least-in-theory-in-cvcityaiai_chooseunit).
-- **Siege production — conditional minimization (trebuchets especially)**: In `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit`, added targeted checks to keep **siege output** as low as needed—**up to none at all** when conditions say they’re poor value (e.g., when the AI is **weaker** or not preparing **city assaults**). Rationale: **trebuchets** are very **hammer-inefficient** outside direct city attacks; **catapults/cannons** also aren’t versatile enough (and are often misused) for defense. Current tuning intentionally leans **low** versus before (but not permanently zero when other conditions are met). Result: **more varied offensive stacks** and **stronger defense** when behind. See [KI#53.3](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#533-tremendously-improved-ai-overbuilding-siege-units-in-particular-trebuchets-but-not-only-where-they-are-detrimental-to-do-build-in-particular-when-weaker-but-not-only). **Update:** Thresholds have been **slightly relaxed pre-Renaissance**; **extra-relaxed when holding key strategic bonuses** (**Iron**, **Copper**, **Horse**, **Camel**, **Elephants**) to enable offense windows (e.g., longbow + siege); and **heavily relaxed from Renaissance onward** so **Cannons** can serve as a defensive pivot and the AI avoids over-producing Pikemen into Cuirassiers, and more generally since later sieges units are stronger and more effective, to also have a more varied and effective military composition.
-- **City-counter role pruning (e.g. Macemen)**: Removed `UNITAI_CITY_COUNTER` from **non-versatile** units (e.g., `UNIT_MACEMAN`). For example for macemen, as city defenders they’re too easy to punish—knights counter them hard, and even crossbow stacks can beat defending macemen—so they’re better assigned to **offensive** `UnitAI` roles. While macemen beat pikemen, pikes seldom assault cities efficiently, so the city-counter slot isn’t warranted. The same adjustment applies to any other units with this mismatch.
-- **Mounted "Counter" role pruning:** Removed `UNITAI_COUNTER` from the mounted units (`UNIT_CHARIOT`, `UNIT_EGYPTIAN_WAR_CHARIOT`, `UNIT_CARTHAGE_NUMIDIAN_CAVALRY`) that had it. Rationale: mounted units don’t "receive defense bonuses" (note: not related to bonuses/ressources) and can be countered by other units; assigning them to COUNTER can park them in cities (e.g. seemingly seen in autoplay with War Chariots) and occupies a slot **Longbowmen** are more fit to fill as they unlock early and are cheap and versatile defenders, so these mounted unit lines are better used for offense roles rather during its window—or skipped outside it.
-- **Stack attack order — spend expendables first:** stack attacks now prefer **bombard/collateral** and **low-effective-strength/low-XP** units first (once the stack commits to attack), preserving elite finishers. Rationale: economically efficient (older/weaker units cost upkeep but scale poorly) and strategically safer (keep high-value units for decisive blows or retreats). Change in `CvSelectionGroupAI::AI_getBestGroupAttacker`. Toggle via SAS defines for A/B testing. See [KI#89](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#89---improved-in-an-attack-stack-attack-with-lower-value-effective-strength-xp-health-units-first).
-- **Great Generals — prefer Military Instructor, focus on hammer hubs:** AI now favours settling Great Generals as **Military Instructors** over attaching them as unit leaders, as the instructor bonus scales better into the late game. **Targeting:** strong bias to the **top 3 hammer cities** with **decreasing weights** (top-1 most, then top-2/3 progressively less). Extra preference if the city **has / is building** the **Heroic Epic effect** building. **Limit removed:** cities may host **multiple** Military Instructors (no per-city cap). Implemented in `CvCityAI::AI_permanentSpecialistValue` and `CvUnitAI::AI_generalMove`. See [KI#69](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#69---tremendously-improved-ai-going-for-great-general-leaders-while-military-instructors-are-much-better-with-added-logic-to-favour-top-hammer-cities-remove-military-instructor-per-city-limit-favour-it-further-if-have-or-building-heroic-epic-effect-building-etc).
-- Update: (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6032+) Rarely in games, for example in save file 450 with France AI, a Great General could be attached to a low-value Longbowman at ~T130. BBAI logging showed the cause was Military Instructor joining was blocked by safety/path gates. To fix it, Great General unit-attachment branches now use tunable minimum target gates that default very high, so Great Generals effectively keep preferring Military Instructor or Military Academy unless these thresholds are deliberately lowered. In the retest after fix, those low-value Longbowman attachments were rejected (and another Great General attachment to USA in the same run that newly occurred as well at ~T180 was successfully blocked) and the Great Generals joined cities as Military Instructors instead. See [KI#69 (Update)](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#69---tremendously-improved-ai-going-for-great-general-leaders-while-military-instructors-are-much-better-with-added-logic-to-favour-top-hammer-cities-remove-military-instructor-per-city-limit-favour-it-further-if-have-or-building-heroic-epic-effect-building-etc).
 - **Unit upgrades - stop wasting gold on tiny gains:** Fix AI tendency to **over-upgrade units** even when the upgrade is **poor value** (high gold cost for a small strength gain), which can burn many turns of **gold** and **slow tech pace**. Before allowing an upgrade, the AI must pass **simple ROI gates** based on **strength** gained vs gold cost: (1) gold per 10% strength gain, and (2) gold per +1 **flat strength** gain; both gates are **more lenient with higher unit EXP** (high-EXP units keep their promotions after being upgraded which is valuable, so spending more gold on them is more justified), while low-EXP units are treated more strictly (often better to let them die and rebuild if needed, unless it is cheap enough to upgrade them). Example (Knight -> Cuirassier): 10 strength -> 13 strength; if one of the gates requires `15 gold per +1 flat strength gain` with `+1 gold per +1 flat strength gain per 1 EXP`, then we won't upgrade the unit if its upgrade cost is more than `15 * 3 = 45 gold` at **0 EXP**, or `((15 + 18) * 3) = 99 gold` at **18 EXP** for this gate. In autoplay, this seemingly leads to **all AIs teching significantly faster**, especially in the **late game** where there are many units and AI would often have its tech pace stall for dozens of turns. Change in `CvUnit::canUpgrade` (single global gate, like `CvUnit::canScrap`); see [KI#88](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#88---tremendously-improved-ai-always-upgrading-way-too-much-units-and-not-teching-at-all-sometimes-for-dozen-turns).
 - **Unit upgrades - prefer the important domain for the map:** AI unit-upgrade value now optionally uses the existing **SAS map-script heaviness** logic to **relatively value** unit upgrades (not a hard ban unless relative value is set high): the real gold price is unchanged; this makes the AI think actual upgrade cost is higher for these unit upgrades. As a result, on **land-heavy maps, naval upgrades are treated as lower priority**, so the AI is more likely to **spend gold upgrading land units first** instead of modernizing old ships that have little strategic value on that map. On **naval-heavy maps, the same idea can apply in reverse for land upgrades**.
 - **Remote captured-city attack stacks:** large AI attack **stacks in faraway captured cities no longer let upgrade waiting override a ready offensive target**. If the stack is large enough, has a valid target, and the target is not too strong, it keeps using the existing attack movement logic **instead of parking most of the empire's army far from its core** because several units could upgrade, which was very problematic. In the BBAI-log sample, the Celtic Boston trapped-stack pattern was visible from T156 to T256; the stricter remote-trap diagnostic confirmed it from T172 to T214, with up to 41 units / 62% of Celtic military parked in a faraway captured city while still having ready targets. See [KI#155](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#155---fixed-base-advciv-issue-remote-captured-city-attack-stacks-could-park-for-many-turns-because-upgrade-waiting-overrode-a-ready-offensive-target).
